-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----
Hash: SHA512

annotations:
  artifacthub.io/changes: |
    - kind: changed
      description: "Updated the edge image to 4.10.5. A 502 from the edge now always means your origin failed. The edge used to pass on every 502 from behind it, including failures of the install: the backend unable to reach the fetcher, the fetcher shedding load (x-origin-status 4) or failing a TIFF conversion (2), and a proxy in front of a remote backend. Those are now answered as 503 (Retry-After: 1), or 504 when the backend's own wait for the fetcher ran out, so they land in a general 5xx alert instead of an origin-error alert. The x-origin-status and x-origin-reason headers stay on the response."
    - kind: added
      description: "imageengine_edge_upstream_502_total{to,reason} counts every 502 the edge received from the install, by the status it answered (to) and why (reason: customer_origin, no_origin_status, backend_timeout, imageengine_failure, …). TROUBLESHOOTING.md explains each reason."
    - kind: fixed
      description: "The edge's imageengine_edge_requests_total{method} label is now GET, HEAD, OPTIONS or other. It used to be whatever method token the client sent, so a scanner could create a new metric series per invented method and grow the edge's memory and scrape size without bound."
    - kind: changed
      description: "Updated the Varnish image to 9.1.0-2. ie-varnish-autoconfig, the backend-discovery sidecar process in every Varnish pod, leaked about 1 MiB an hour for as long as the pod ran (a shard up for two weeks carried about 350 MiB of it against its memory limit). Fixed; long-lived shards no longer creep towards their memory limit."
    - kind: fixed
      description: "imageengine_varnish_memory_limit_bytes follows an in-place resize of the Varnish container. It read POD_MEMORY_LIMIT first, which is fixed when the container starts; it now reads the live cgroup limit first and uses POD_MEMORY_LIMIT only when no cgroup limit can be read."
    - kind: fixed
      description: "TROUBLESHOOTING.md: rewrote \"502 Bad Gateway from the edge\". A 502 means your origin failed; the edge and Varnish never answer 502 for their own failures. The section explains each x-origin-status value, how the edge answers failures of the install (503/504, with the reason in imageengine_edge_upstream_502_total), and how to alert on 502 separately from other 5xx."
